        "problem": "Solve the inequality $5\\cdot {2}^{x}<{3}^{2-x}.$",
        "worked_solution": "Taking the natural logarithm of both sides and applying the laws of logarithms, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5\\cdot {2}^{x} & <{3}^{2-x} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}\u2061(5\\cdot {2}^{x}) & <\\operatorname{ln}\u2061({3}^{2-x}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}\u20615+\\operatorname{ln}\u2061({2}^{x}) & <\\operatorname{ln}\u2061({3}^{2-x}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}\u20615+x\\operatorname{ln}\u20612 & <(2-x)\\operatorname{ln}\u20613 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}\u20615+x\\operatorname{ln}\u20612 & <2\\operatorname{ln}\u20613-x\\operatorname{ln}\u20613 \\\\ x\\operatorname{ln}\u20612+x\\operatorname{ln}\u20613 & <2\\operatorname{ln}\u20613-\\operatorname{ln}\u20615 \\\\ x(\\operatorname{ln}\u20612+\\operatorname{ln}\u20613) & <2\\operatorname{ln}\u20613-\\operatorname{ln}\u20615 \\\\ x & <\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}\u20613-\\operatorname{ln}\u20615}{\\operatorname{ln}\u20612+\\operatorname{ln}\u20613}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
